/* Thread B later accesses it via a shared pointer */
/* No lock needed because both threads have their own refcount fields;
   the object lives as long as either thread holds a reference. */
```
- Each thread maintains its own counter; when a thread drops its last reference, it frees the object immediately.
- When another thread still holds a reference, no action is taken until that thread releases its own reference.
### Interaction with Existing Code
- Most built‑ins remain unchanged; only internal helpers that previously relied on global state are refactored to use per‑thread data structures.
- The interpreter loop itself does not change; only how objects are tracked differs.
